  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Тетрадь битмейкера",
    "overview": "The guide is a beatmaker's study notebook kept next to the device. Its pages are ruled and orderly: numbered lessons, step grids drawn like squared paper, chord sketches on a one-octave keyboard, short fact lists with the term on its own line. Reading comes first; nothing on the page competes with the instruction the learner is following with the P-6 in hand.\n\nThe device lives in the details, the way stickers and margin sketches live in a real notebook. Button names are drawn as small graphite key chips with a hard bottom edge, display readouts as red seven-segment chips on dark glass, and the panel explorer as a faithful drawing of the hardware. These are the only raised, tactile things on the page: the interface is quiet, the instrument is tactile.\n\nOne accent carries meaning. Teal \"step light\" marks what is on: the current tab, a lit sequencer step, a link to follow, the trunk of the branch tree. Pages are flat; depth is two tonal steps of paper, a hairline rule and nothing else. Density stays calm on phones: no card chrome below 560px, hairline dividers instead.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Notebook structure first: numbered lessons, ruled step grids, fact lists, one-octave chord sketches.",
      "Hardware details as the only relief: graphite key chips and red LCD chips inside running text, a faithful panel drawing.",
      "One meaningful accent, \"step light\", for on, current and link.",
      "Flat paper surfaces in two tones with hairline rules; no shadows anywhere.",
      "Six type sizes, three families with fixed roles: display, reading, device."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Lit Step Rule",
        "body": "Step Light means \"on, current or follow this\". It is never decoration: no accent headings, borders or backgrounds without a state behind them.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Display Is The Device Rule",
        "body": "LCD Red appears only where the P-6 display is being reproduced. If it is not something the screen of the device would show, it is not red.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Six Sizes Rule",
        "body": "Text uses the six scale steps (12/14/16/18/22 px and the fluid title). Step-grid labels (9–11 px) are the only exception, because 16 columns must fit a phone.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Device Voice Rule",
        "body": "Mono type means \"this is on the P-6 or about the P-6 grid\". Do not set prose in mono.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Notebook Rhythm Rule",
        "body": "One idea per line group: a short heading on its own line, the explanation under it, space or a hairline before the next. Never a bold lead-in buried in a paragraph run.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hardware-Only Relief Rule",
        "body": "Only elements that depict the P-6 (key chips, LCD chips, the panel drawing) may have bottom edges, glows or inset rings. Interface components stay flat; pressed feedback is a brightness change, never a lift or shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
